<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Mosque of Demak</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Masjid Agung Demak</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Mosque of Demak, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-java">Central Java</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, is one of the oldest mosques in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, traditionally said to have been built in the late 15th century by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wali-songo">Wali Songo</a>, the nine saints who spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> on the island. It has a Javanese tiered roof rather than a dome, and one of its main pillars is said to have been made of wood chips by Sunan Kalijaga. It remains a major pilgrimage site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-demak.jpg" alt="Great Mosque of Demak" width="320" height="182" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Astayoga at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Masjid_demak.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
